.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct \- type-safe hash map construction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chashmap.h>

chmap_construct(hm_name, key_type, val_type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-safe macro for creating a hash map with default settings.
Automatically determines key and value types and creates type marker
variables for compile-time type checking.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name   Name of the hash map variable to create
key_type  Type of keys
val_type  Type of values
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Terminates program on allocation failure
Creates hidden type marker variables for type safety
Uses DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets
Uses default memory management and hashing
.fi
.SH "FLOAT AND DOUBLE KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
For a float or double key_type, key equality is bitwise equality,
not IEEE 754 == equality, with one exception: -0.0 and 0.0 are
canonicalized to the same key, matching -0.0 == 0.0 in C.
Every other bit pattern, including NaN, follows bitwise equality:
a NaN key is always found again by the exact bit pattern it was
inserted with (unlike NaN == NaN, which is always false in C), and
two different NaN payloads are two different keys.
.fi
.SH "LONG DOUBLE KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
For a long double key_type, key equality is based on numeric value
(native ==), not bitwise equality: -0.0L and 0.0L are the same key,
and two keys holding the identical value remain the same key even
if their platform-defined padding bits differ. Every NaN long
double collapses into a single key, unlike float/double's own
distinct-NaN-payload behavior. This value-based handling is
bypassed when a custom hashing function is supplied at creation
time; see chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(map, int, char*);
chmap_insert(map, 42, "hello");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_construct_full (3),
.BR chmap_create (3)
